  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "R.Vaithilingam vs The Food Safety Officer on 4 December, 2019 Author: G.R.Swaminathan Bench: G.R.Swaminathan Crl.O.P.(MD)Nos.13576 and 19449 Before The Madurai Bench Of Madras High Court Dated: 04.12.2019 Coram The Honourable Mr.Justice G.R.Swaminathan Crl Op(Md). Nos.13576 and 19449 of 2015 and M.P.(MD)Nos.1 and 2 of 2015 R.Vaithilingam ... Petitioner in Crl.O.P. (MD)No.13756 of 2015 R.Chandramohan ... Petitioner in Crl.O.P. (MD)No.19449 of 2015 -Vs- The Food Safety Officer, Ambasamudram Blcok, Government Hospital Road, Ambasmudram 627 401. ... Respondent in both Crl.O.P Common Prayer: Criminal Original Petitions filed under Section 482 of Cr.P.C, to call for the records in C.C.No.264 of 2014 and C.C.No.49 of 2015 respectively, on the file of the Judicial Magistrate, Ambasamudram and quash the same insofar as the petitioners are concerned. (in both Crl.O.Ps) For Petitioner : Mr.M.Rajaraman",
    "facts": "The petitioners are manufacturers of gingelly oil. The respondent obtained samples of gingelly oil said to have been sold by the petitioners herein to a retailer by name Natarajan, Propretor of Sri Vinayagar Store, Ambasamuthuram, on 23.01.2014 at about 11.30 a.m. The respondent sent the samples for test analysis to the Food Analyst, Palayamkottai. The report dated 19.02.2014 sent by the Food Analyst concluded that the sample is misbranded and is also substandard. Based on the report, the respondent filed the impugned private complaint not only against the retailers but also against the manufactures namely., the petitioners herein. Cognizance of the offences under Sections 51, 52, 55 r/w. Sections 3(1)(ZF), 3(1)(Zx), 26(1), 26(2)(2)(5), 27(1) and 27(2)(c) of the Food Safety and Standards Act r/w. Section 2.2.1(3), 2.2.2(4) and 8 of Food Safety and Standards (Packaging and Labelling) Regulations, 2001 and Section 2.2.1.(12) of Food Safety and Standards (Food Products Standards and Food Additives) Regulation, 2011, was taken and summons were issued to the accused. To quash the same, these criminal original petitions came to be filed. http://www.judis.nic.in Crl.O.P.(MD)Nos.13576 and 19449 of 2015",
    "arguments": "2.The learned counsel appearing for the petitioners submitted that the respondent failed to serve a copy of the report of the Food Analyst and as a result, they were deprived of the valuable right of filing statutory appeal. On that ground, the petitioners want the impugned proceedings to be [LEAKAGE_MASK]. 3.Per contra, the learned Government Advocate (Crl. Side) submitted that the report in question was duly served on the retailers from whom the samples were taken. The learned Government Advocate (Crl. Side) would contend that there is no mandate to serve a copy of the report on the manufacturer. In this regard, the learned Government Advocate (Crl. Side) placed reliance on the Rule 2(4)(2)(6) of the Food Safety and Standards Rules, 2011. Which reads as follows:- 6.The Designated Officer shall keep two copies of analysis report for further action, one copy shall be sent to Food Safety Officer for record andn one copy to Food business Operatior from whom the sample was taken. http://www.judis.nic.in Crl.O.P.(MD)Nos.13576 and 19449 of 2015 4.It is true that the requirement of the rule is that the Designated Officer should get two copies of the analysis report for further action, send one copy to the Food Safety Officer for record and one copy to the food business operator from whom the sample was taken. Of course, the learned Government Advocate (Crl. Side) is right in his contention that the aforesaid rule casts a duty to send a copy only to the person from whom the sample was taken. 5.The expression ''food business operator'' has been defined in Section 3(O) of the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006, as follows:- ''(O) 'food business operator' in relation to food business means a person by whom R.Vaithilingam vs The Food Safety Officer on 4 December, 2019 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/136586468/ 2 the business is carried on or owned and is responsible for ensuring the complance of this Act, rules and regulations made thereunder,'' 6.The definition of the term ''food business operator'' is rather wide. 8.As rightly contended by the learned counsel appearing for petitioners, the retailer can escape from his liability by simply taking a stand that he sold the article in the same condition in which it was received by him and that he had no knowledge that it was unsafe. 9.As per Section 46(4) of the Act, an appeal against the report of the Food Analyst shall lie before the Designated Officer, if he so decides to refer the matter to the referral food laboratory as notified by the Food Authority for opinion. http://www.judis.nic.in Crl.O.P.(MD)Nos.13576 and 19449 of 2015 11.I 12.In fact while interpreting the earlier statue i.e., Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954, the Hon'ble Supreme Court in the decision reported in (2019) SCC 1310 (M/s.Alkem Laboratories Ltd., v. State of Madhaya Pradesh) held that the accused has to be given prior notice that the samples of food articles manufactured and sold by them have been sent for analysis before the Public Analyst prepares their report. Only if this requirement is fulfilled, the accused can be said to have a fair trial.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "2.The learned counsel appearing for the petitioners submitted that the respondent failed to serve a copy of the report of the Food Analyst and as a result, they were deprived of the valuable right of filing statutory appeal. On that ground, the petitioners want the impugned proceedings to be [LEAKAGE_MASK]. 3.Per contra, the learned Government Advocate (Crl. Side) submitted that the report in question was duly served on the retailers from whom the samples were taken. The learned Government Advocate (Crl. Side) would contend that there is no mandate to serve a copy of the report on the manufacturer. In this regard, the learned Government Advocate (Crl. Side) placed reliance on the Rule 2(4)(2)(6) of the Food Safety and Standards Rules, 2011. is true that the requirement of the rule is that the Designated Officer should get two copies of the analysis report for further action, send one copy to the Food Safety Officer for record and one copy to the food business operator from whom the sample was taken. Of course, the learned Government Advocate (Crl. Side) is right in his contention that the aforesaid rule casts a duty to send a copy only to the person from whom the sample was taken. definition of the term ''food business operator'' is rather wide. 8.As rightly contended by the learned counsel appearing for petitioners, the retailer can escape from his liability by simply taking a stand that he sold the article in the same condition in which it was received by him and that he had no knowledge that it was unsafe.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "Which reads as follows:- 6.The Designated Officer shall keep two copies of analysis report for further action, one copy shall be sent to Food Safety Officer for record andn one copy to Food business Operatior from whom the sample was taken. http://www.judis.nic.in Crl.O.P.(MD)Nos.13576 and 19449 of 2015 4.It 5.The expression ''food business operator'' has been defined in Section 3(O) of the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006, as follows:- ''(O) 'food business operator' in relation to food business means a person by whom R.Vaithilingam vs The Food Safety Officer on 4 December, 2019 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/136586468/ 2 the business is carried on or owned and is responsible for ensuring the complance of this Act, rules and regulations made thereunder,'' 6.The 9.As per Section 46(4) of the Act, an appeal against the report of the Food Analyst shall lie before the Designated Officer, if he so decides to refer the matter to the referral food laboratory as notified by the Food Authority for opinion. http://www.judis.nic.in Crl.O.P.(MD)Nos.13576 and 19449 of 2015 11.I 12.In fact while interpreting the earlier statue i.e., Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954, the Hon'ble Supreme Court in the decision reported in (2019) SCC 1310 (M/s.Alkem Laboratories Ltd., v. State of Madhaya Pradesh) held that the accused has to be given prior notice that the samples of food articles manufactured and sold by them have been sent for analysis before the Public Analyst prepares their report. Only if this requirement is fulfilled, the accused can be said to have a fair trial."
